Selected Idea
-------------

Prefer the official GitHub CLI packages (Debian/RPM/Homebrew) - they're the supported install path
The strongest editorial story the source supports is that GitHub CLI provides official packages (Debian, RPM, Homebrew) that are explicitly maintained by the CLI maintainers and updated via a CI deployment workflow - and these are the recommended, supported installation methods.

Research
--------

### Facts
1. The document contains a "Recommended (Official)" section.
2. The "Recommended (Official)" section contains a "Debian" subsection that states: "Debian packages are hosted on the GitHub CLI marketing site for various operating systems including: Debian, Raspberry Pi, Ubuntu Linux."
3. The Debian subsection states: "These packages are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with updates powered by [GitHub CLI deployment workflow]."
4. The Debian subsection provides the following apt-based install sequence:

```bash
(type -p wget >/dev/null || (sudo apt update && sudo apt install wget -y)) \
	&& sudo mkdir -p -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings \
	&& out=$(mktemp) && wget -nv -O$out https://cli.github.com/packages/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
	&& cat $out | sudo tee /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg > /dev/null \
	&& sudo chmod go+r /etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg \
	&& sudo mkdir -p -m 755 /etc/apt/sources.list.d \
	&& echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/githubcli-archive-keyring.gpg] https://cli.github.com/packages stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/github-cli.list > /dev/null \
	&& sudo apt update \
	&& sudo apt install gh -y
```

5. The Debian subsection provides the following upgrade commands:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install gh
```

6. The "Recommended (Official)" section contains an "RPM" subsection that states: "RPM packages are hosted on the GitHub CLI marketing site for various operating systems including: Amazon Linux 2, CentOS, Fedora, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, openSUSE, SUSE."
7. The RPM subsection states: "These packages are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with updates powered by [GitHub CLI deployment workflow]."
8. The RPM subsection includes a DNF5 note and provides these DNF5 install commands (the source marks these commands as applying to DNF5 only):

```bash
sudo dnf install dnf5-plugins
sudo dnf config-manager addrepo --from-repofile=https://cli.github.com/packages/rpm/gh-cli.repo
sudo dnf install gh --repo gh-cli
```

9. The RPM subsection includes a DNF4 note and provides these DNF4 install commands (the source marks these commands as applying to DNF4 only):

```bash
sudo dnf install 'dnf-command(config-manager)'
sudo dnf config-manager --add-repo https://cli.github.com/packages/rpm/gh-cli.repo
sudo dnf install gh --repo gh-cli
```

10. The RPM subsection provides Amazon Linux 2 (yum) install commands:

```bash
type -p yum-config-manager >/dev/null || sudo yum install yum-utils
sudo yum-config-manager --add-repo https://cli.github.com/packages/rpm/gh-cli.repo
sudo yum install gh
```

11. The RPM subsection provides openSUSE/SUSE (zypper) install commands:

```bash
sudo zypper addrepo https://cli.github.com/packages/rpm/gh-cli.repo
sudo zypper ref
sudo zypper install gh
```

12. The "Recommended (Official)" section contains a "Homebrew" subsection that states: "Homebrew is a free and open-source software package management system that simplifies the installation of software on Apple's operating system, macOS, as well as Linux."
13. The Homebrew subsection states: "The GitHub CLI formulae is supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers with help from our friends at Homebrew with updated powered by [homebrew/homebrew-core]."
14. The Homebrew subsection provides the install command:

```bash
brew install gh
```

15. The Homebrew subsection provides the upgrade command:

```bash
brew upgrade gh
```

16. The "Precompiled binaries" subsection states: "GitHub CLI releases contain precompiled binaries for `386`, `amd64`, `arm64`, and `armv6` architectures."
17. The document contains a "Community (Unofficial)" section with an IMPORTANT note that states: "The GitHub CLI team does not maintain the following packages or repositories. We are unable to provide support for these installation methods or any guarantees of stability, security, or availability for these installation methods."
18. The Alpine subsection states: "The GitHub CLI package is supported by the Alpine Linux community with updates powered by alpine/aports." and provides the install command:

```bash
apk add github-cli
```

19. The Android/Termux subsection states: "The GitHub CLI package is supported by the Termux community with updates powered by termux/termux-packages." and provides the install/upgrade command:

```bash
pkg install gh
```

20. The Arch Linux subsection states: "The GitHub CLI package is supported by the Arch Linux community with updates powered by Arch Linux packaging." and provides the install command:

```bash
sudo pacman -S github-cli
```

21. The Conda subsection states: "The GitHub CLI package is supported by the Conda community with updates powered by conda-forge/gh-feedstock." and provides the install command:

```bash
conda install gh --channel conda-forge
```

22. The Webi subsection states: "The GitHub CLI package is supported by the Webi community" and provides the install command and an upgrade example:

```bash
curl -sS https://webi.sh/gh | sh
```

and

```bash
webi gh@stable
```

23. The document contains a "Discouraged" section with a WARNING that states: "The GitHub CLI team actively discourages use of the following methods of installation." and the document specifically states: "our team suggests never installing gh as a snap."

### Interpretations
1. [derived from Facts 1, 2, 6, 12] The document groups Debian, RPM, and Homebrew under a labeled "Recommended (Official)" section.
2. [derived from Facts 3, 7, 13, 16] The document explicitly states that the Debian packages, RPM packages, and Homebrew formulae are supported by the GitHub CLI maintainers and that updates are powered by the project's deployment workflows or homebrew/homebrew-core.
3. [derived from Facts 17 and 18-22] The document distinguishes a set of community-maintained installation methods (Community (Unofficial)) and states that the GitHub CLI team does not maintain those packages and cannot provide support or guarantees for them.

